<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Israel Finkelstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Israel Finkelstein is an Israeli archaeologist at Tel Aviv University and a long-time co-director of the excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Megiddo</a>. He is known for the low chronology, which redates many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> buildings once attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> to the ninth century BCE, and for arguing, notably in The Bible Unearthed (2001, with Neil Asher Silberman), that the united monarchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> and Solomon was a small highland polity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/israel-finkelstein.jpg" alt="Israel Finkelstein" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Argonauter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Israel_Finkelstein.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
